UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 17, Lesson 1: Communicating respectfully', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Respectful communication uses words, tone and body language that show you value the other person. It includes listening without interrupting, using polite words, taking turns, asking questions and disagreeing without put-downs. Tone and body language can change the meaning of words, so a calm voice, eye contact if comfortable and an open posture help. Respect also means accepting different ways of communicating, including signing, writing or a quiet style.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nRewrite ''That''s a stupid idea!'' so that it disagrees respectfully. Add the tone and body language you would use.\n\nFEEDBACK\nRewrite: ''I''m not sure that will work, could we try this instead?'' Tone: calm. Body: face the speaker. A respectful disagreement keeps the idea and removes the insult.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving words are the only thing that matters. Tone of voice and body language also send messages.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCollect three examples of respectful and three of disrespectful communication from a story, a show or your day (without naming real people). For each, name the words, tone and body language and rewrite the disrespectful ones respectfully.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Respectful communication uses words, tone and body language that show you value the other person. It includes listening without interrupting, using polite words, taking turns, asking questions and disagreeing without put-downs. Tone and body language can change the meaning of words, so a calm voice, eye contact if comfortable and an open posture help. Respect also means accepting different ways of communicating, including signing, writing or a quiet style.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn and discuss","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-RRS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Collect three examples of respectful and three of disrespectful communication from a story, a show or your day (without naming real people). For each, name the words, tone and body language and rewrite the disrespectful  Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Rewrite: ''I''m not sure that will work, could we try this instead?'' Tone: calm. Body: face the speaker. A respectful disagreement keeps the idea and removes the insult. Reasoning: I used respectful words by attending to tone of voice rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Believing words are the only thing that matters. Tone of voice and body language also send messages. Correction: Rewrite: ''I''m not sure that will work, could we try this instead?'' Tone: calm. Body: face the speaker. A respectful disagreement keeps the idea and removes… Why: the correction restores accurate use of respectful words.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing words are the only thing that matters. Tone of voice and body language also send messages. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating respectful words on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with respectful words, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Rewrite: ''I''m not sure that will work, could we try this instead?'' Tone: calm. Body: face the speaker. A respectful disagreement keeps the idea and removes… This matches the lesson skill for respectful words.","options":["Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Rewrite: ''I''m not sure that will work, could we try this instead?'' Tone: calm. Body: face the speaker. A respectful disagreement keeps the idea and removes…","Believing words are the only thing that matters. Tone of voice and body language also send messages."],"question":"Rewrite ''That''s a stupid idea!'' so that it disagrees respectfully. Add the tone and body language you would use. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Believing words are the only thing that matters. Tone of voice and body language also send messages. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Rewrite: ''I''m not sure that will work, could we try this instead?'' Tone: calm. Body: face the speaker. A respectful disagreement keeps the idea and removes…","Careful use of tone of voice with clear evidence.","Believing words are the only thing that matters. Tone of voice and body language also send messages.","Respectful communication uses words, tone and body language that show you value the other person. It includes listening without interrupting, using polite…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising respectful words?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named tone of voice, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the tone of voice used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising respectful words. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Rewrite: ''I''m not sure that will work, could we try this instead?'' Tone: calm. Body: face the speaker. A respectful disagreement keeps the idea and removes… uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Believing words are the only thing that matters. Tone of voice and body language also send messages.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Rewrite: ''I''m not sure that will work, could we try this instead?'' Tone: calm. Body: face the speaker. A respectful disagreement keeps the idea and removes…"],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about respectful words?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Believing words are the only thing that matters. Tone of voice and body language also send messages.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about respectful words."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about tone of voice?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: two students disagree about a game. Respectful: ''I see it differently. Can I tell you my idea?'' Tone: calm. Body: facing the…","Believing words are the only thing that matters. Tone of voice and body language also send messages.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Respectful communication uses words, tone and body language that show you value the other person. It… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Believing words are the only thing that matters. Tone of voice and body language also send messages.","Collect three examples of respectful and three of disrespectful communication from a story, a show or your day (without naming real people). For…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of respectful words?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating respectful words on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Rewrite: ''I''m not sure that will work, could we try this instead?'' Tone: calm. Body: face the speaker. A respectful disagreement keeps the idea and removes the insult. Reasoning: I used respectful words by attending to tone of voice rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with respectful words, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Believing words are the only thing that matters. Tone of voice and body language also send messages. Correction: Rewrite: ''I''m not sure that will work, could we try this instead?'' Tone: calm. Body: face the speaker. A respectful disagreement keeps the idea and removes… Why: the correction restores accurate use of respectful words.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w17-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise respectful words.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Health, Wellbeing and Relationships","success_criteria":["I can use respectful words with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify tone of voice and explain why it matters.","I can practise body language independently on a new prompt.","I can notice disagreeing politely needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Respectful communication uses words, tone and body language that show you value the other person. It includes listening without interrupting, using polite words, taking turns, asking questions and disagreeing without put-downs. Tone and body language can change the meaning of…","options":["A guess with no reason.","Respectful communication uses words, tone and body language that show you value the other person. It includes listening without interrupting, using polite words, taking turns, asking questions and disagreeing without put-downs. Tone and body language can change the meaning of words, so a calm voice, eye contact if comfortable and an open posture help. Respect also means accepting different ways of communicating, including signing, writing or a quiet style.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about respectful words?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: two students disagree about a game. Respectful: ''I see it differently. Can I tell you my idea?'' Tone: calm. Body: facing the person. Disrespectful: rolling eyes and saying ''that''s a dumb idea''. The same disagreement is handled with respect when words, tone and body language all match.\n\nTRY FIRST\nRewrite ''That''s a stupid idea!'' so that it disagrees respectfully. Add the tone and body language you would use.\n\nFEEDBACK\nRewrite: ''I''m not sure that will work, could we try this instead?'' Tone: calm. Body: face the speaker. A respectful disagreement keeps the idea and removes the insult.","explain":"Respectful communication uses words, tone and body language that show you value the other person. It includes listening without interrupting, using polite words, taking turns, asking questions and disagreeing without put-downs. Tone and body language can change the meaning of words, so a calm voice, eye contact if comfortable and an open posture help. Respect also means accepting different ways of communicating, including signing, writing or a quiet style.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing words are the only thing that matters. Tone of voice and body language also send messages. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Respectful and put-down words.","caption":"Remove the insult.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Respectful</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">I see it differently</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Put-down</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">that is a dumb idea</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: two students disagree about a game. Respectful: ''I see it differently. Can I tell you my idea?'' Tone: calm. Bod Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: two students disagree about a game. Respectful: ''I see it differently. Can I tell you my idea?'' Tone: calm. Bod Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about respectful words?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: two students disagree about a game. Respectful: ''I see it differently. Can I tell you my idea?'' Tone: calm. Body: facing the person. Disrespectful: rolling eyes and saying ''that''s a dumb idea''. The same disagreement is handled with respect when words, tone and body language all match. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Tone and body language.","caption":"All three match.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Tone</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">calm</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Body</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">face the speaker</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about respectful words?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving words are the only thing that matters. Tone of voice and body language also send messages. Here is a more helpful example: Example: two students disagree about a game. Respectful: ''I see it differently. Can I tell you my idea?'' Tone: calm. Body: facing the person. Disrespectful: rolling eyes and saying ''that''s a dumb idea''. The same disagreement is handled with respect when words, tone and body language all match.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nRewrite: ''I''m not sure that will work, could we try this instead?'' Tone: calm. Body: face the speaker. A respectful disagreement keeps the idea and removes the insult.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing words are the only thing that matters. Tone of voice and body language also send messages. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about respectful words?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRespectful communication uses words, tone and body language that show you value the other person. It includes listening without interrupting, using polite words, taking turns, asking questions and disagreeing without put-downs. Tone and body language can change the meaning of words, so a calm voice, eye contact if comfortable and an open posture help. Respect also means accepting different ways of communicating, including signing, writing or a quiet style. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 17, Lesson 1: Communicating respectfully","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Friendships and respect","week":17,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: two students disagree about a game. Respectful: ''I see it differently. Can I tell you my idea?'' Tone: calm. Body: facing the person. Disrespectful: rolling eyes and saying ''that''s a dumb idea''. The same disagreement is handled with respect when words, tone and body language all match.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Respectful and put-down words.","caption":"Remove the insult.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Respectful</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">I see it differently</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Put-down</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">that is a dumb idea</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Tone and body language.","caption":"All three match.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Tone</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">calm</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\"